/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Navigation starts with a model of the Earth.
   - The real Earth is flattened at the poles, so satellite navigation uses the WGS-84 datum: an Earth-centred coordinate system plus a reference ellipsoid (semi-major axis a = 6 378 137 m, flattening f = 1/298.257223563).
   - A great circle is the shortest path on the surface between two points; its plane passes through the Earth's centre.
   - The nautical mile (nm) is 1852 m, first defined as one minute of arc of latitude.
   - Δσ is the angle between the two points seen from the Earth's centre, in radians; φ₁ and φ₂ are the two latitudes; Δλ is the difference of longitude; R ≈ 6371 km is the mean Earth radius; d is the great-circle distance in the units of R.
   - Heading is where the nose points, measured clockwise from north.
   - Track is the direction the aircraft actually moves over the ground.
   - Bearing is the direction of one point from another, so always name both points.
   - A radial is a line leaving a station, named by its magnetic bearing from that station.
   - A line of position (LOP) is the set of all points where one measurement would give the same reading.
*/
