/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - A point-source aid has exactly one transmitter, at a surveyed position.
   - The automatic direction finder (ADF) works with a non-directional beacon (NDB), a simple transmitter in the low and medium frequency bands, a few hundred kilohertz, radiating equally in all directions.
   - The very high frequency omnidirectional range (VOR) transmits at 108–117.95 MHz.
   - Distance measuring equipment (DME) is two-way ranging at 962–1213 MHz.
   - d is the ground range in metres or nautical miles; r is the measured slant range; h is the aircraft height above the transponder, in the same units as d.
   - The gap matters close in.
   - Pair a VOR with a co-located DME and one station gives both bearing and range: a rho-theta fix, ρ for range and θ for bearing.
   - Any two-line fix is only as good as its cut angle — the angle at which the lines cross.
   - A hyperbolic aid measures no distance at all.
   - Δr is the range difference in metres; c = 3 × 10⁸ m/s is the speed of light; Δt is the measured time difference in seconds.
*/
