/* book exam traps: One hyperbola is one line of position, not a fix. Two hyperbolas, hence three stations, are needed; a two-station reading fixes nothing. | DME reads slant range. Directly overhead at 10 km altitude the DME shows 10 km, not zero, and the ground distance is always smaller than the reading. | A VOR radial runs from the station to the aircraft. Radial 090 puts you east of the station and your track to the station is 270; swap the two and the fix is 180° wrong. | A hyperbolic receiver's clock need not be synchronised with the stations; only the stations must agree with each other, because a common clock offset cancels in the difference. Satellite navigation instead solves that offset as an extra unknown. | Watch units in Δr = c·Δt: microseconds, not milliseconds. A 100 ms difference would give 30 000 km, larger than the earth. */
